  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">WFP BSFP Nutrition project.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Marsabit is a semi-arid area located in a drought prone region of the country and which currently falls under the Integrated Phase Classification (IPC) of red. It has very poor annual rainfall characterized with limited pasture and crop failure. The recent rains have not been very reliable and this has resulted to high malnutrition rates of 22.7 and severe malnutrition rates of 5.0.

The whole of Marsabit District is food insecure, suffering from chronic food shortage for most of the year. Relief food is a major source of food as crop products are not sufficient to supply the entire district. Coupled with poverty, and the increase of food prices, households do not have sufficient funds to purchase the little food on the Market.

CAFOD in partnership with the Catholic Diocese of Marsabit expressed an interest to implement Blanket Supplementary Feeding Project (BSFP) in Laisamis and Loiyangallani Districts of Marsabit County. The proposed action is to provide immediate emergency support in nutrition to reach vulnerable households through strategies that prevent acute malnutrition through provision of supplementary Corn Soya Blend (CSB) and fortified vegetable oil. Target beneficiaries are all children less than 3 years, pregnant and lactating mothers. 

Beneficiaries will receive 200gm of CSB and 20 gm of vegetable oil per person per day for 5 months. This proposed project is complemented by other actions, including improving access to routine medication and boosting immunization and vitamin A coverage through providing routine medication to all beneficiaries of BSFP. The project will ensure that individual beneficiaries and communities can have a better and improved quality of life through this integrated approach.

The project is directly targeting 9,277 individual beneficiaries of whom 5,455 are female and 3,822 are male.</narrative>
  </description>
